// =============================================================================
// Traffic Light State Machine
//
// A minimal first example: an enum plus a switch.
//
// An "enum" is a type with a fixed, named set of values. Here a traffic light
// can only ever be Red, Yellow, or Green — nothing else is allowed.
// =============================================================================

enum TrafficLight
    Red
    Yellow
    Green
end enum

// "switch" branches on the exact value of an enum. It must handle every
// variant (or provide a "default" arm), so the compiler can check you have not
// forgotten one. This function answers "which light comes next?" for a simple
// cycle: Red → Green → Yellow → Red → …
//
// `copy` means "pass by value": the function gets its own copy of `light` and
// cannot change the caller's value.
fn next(copy light as TrafficLight) returns TrafficLight
    switch light
        case Red: return Green
        case Yellow: return Red
        case Green: return Yellow
    end switch
end fn

// Start at Red and step through five transitions. The `{light}` and
// `{next(light)}` parts embed a value into the text. The loop counts from 1
// to 5 (Lale is 1-based, like mathematics); `_` is a throwaway name for the
// loop counter, which the body never uses.
var light as TrafficLight = Red
loop var _ as u32 from 1 to 5
    write "{light} → {next(light)}"
    light = next(light)
end loop
